Omit OS — licensing overview
============================

Omit OS is one repository under several licences, one per kind of
component. Every source file names its licence in its first lines
(SPDX-License-Identifier); files that cannot carry a notice are covered
by REUSE.toml. The full text of every licence is in the LICENSES/
directory. This page is a summary in plain words, not a licence and not
legal advice; where it and a file's own notice disagree, the notice
counts.

Which licence applies where
---------------------------

  Kernel side — GPL-2.0-only
      kernel/                 the kernel, its drivers, the Linux
                              compatibility runtime
      compat/linux-kpi/       the Linux compatibility headers
      shell/                  the shell (it is linked into the kernel)
      boot/                   the UEFI boot loader
      libs/acpi  libs/bootproto  libs/elf  libs/ext2  libs/fat
      libs/gpt   libs/hwdb       libs/net  libs/term libs/vfs

  The system call interface — GPL-2.0-only WITH the Omit OS syscall note
      libs/abi/               system call numbers, error codes and the
                              structures exchanged with the kernel

  Imported Linux drivers — as upstream (GPL-2.0-or-later)
      drivers/linux/          unmodified files from the Linux kernel;
                              their own notices apply and are not touched

  User-space system components — GPL-3.0-or-later
      userspace/programs/     the desktop and the bundled programs,
                              including the Windows compatibility layer
      compat/windows/pe/  compat/windows/win32/
                              libraries of that layer

  The SDK: what other people's programs link — MIT OR Apache-2.0
      userspace/rt/           the runtime of every user program
      libs/window/            the window protocol
      libs/gfx/               drawing and text
      libs/mm/                the memory allocator (also used by the
                              kernel, under MIT)

  Build tools, tests fixtures and CI — MIT OR Apache-2.0
      tools/  .github/  compat/windows/samples/
      Cargo.toml files, Cargo.lock and other build configuration

  Documentation — CC-BY-SA-4.0
      README.md, docs/, this file, CONTRIBUTING.md, TRADEMARKS.md,
      IP_POLICY.md, PROVENANCE.md

  Third-party material keeps its own licence. The console and desktop
  font is Noto Sans Mono (SIL Open Font License 1.1), which comes in
  through the noto-sans-mono-bitmap crate and is not stored in this
  repository. docs/THIRD_PARTY.md lists every third-party crate.

The system call exception
-------------------------

A program that uses the kernel only through its system calls and the
inter-process communication it provides is not a derived work of the
kernel, and may be under any licence. The files in libs/abi/, which a
program has to build against to make those calls, carry an explicit
exception saying so (LICENSES/LicenseRef-OmitOS-syscall-note.txt; it is
modelled on the Linux kernel's). The exception covers nothing else:
code that copies or links other kernel-side code is under GPL-2.0-only.

Why it is divided this way
--------------------------

The kernel can run unmodified Linux drivers, which are mostly under
GPL-2.0-only; everything linked into the kernel must therefore be
compatible with that licence. GPL-3.0, LGPL-3.0, AGPL and
Apache-2.0-only code is not, and must not be linked into it. Code under
"MIT OR Apache-2.0" is used there under MIT.

Kernel and user space share no source files: they meet at the system
call interface. Code must not be copied between components whose
licences are incompatible (for example from the kernel into the SDK, or
from the desktop into the kernel).

The build tool in tools/ links two kernel-side libraries (libs/vfs and
libs/gpt), so the tool as built is a GPL-2.0-only work, although its own
source files are under MIT OR Apache-2.0. It is not part of the system
image.

Names and logos
---------------

The licences above cover code and documentation. They do not grant any
right to the project's name; see TRADEMARKS.md.
